Outputs of the commands that a visitor runs to trace the route to the server, for tests/e2e/better-route-back-parse.spec.js.
Each NAME.txt has a NAME.expected.json: what the parser must give (checked by hand against the text).

linux-*, REAL: run on Linux (Ubuntu), here. The hostname of mtr was changed to "host".
  linux-traceroute-n.txt        traceroute -n -m 12 -w 1 -q 3 1.1.1.1  (lost probes, three routers in one hop)
  linux-traceroute-names.txt    traceroute -m 10 -w 1 8.8.8.8  (names and "(address)", three routers in one hop)
  linux-tracepath-n.txt         tracepath -n -m 12 1.1.1.1  (the same hop twice, "pmtu", "asymm", "no reply")
  linux-mtr.txt                 mtr -rwn -c 3 -m 12 1.1.1.1
  linux-traceroute-v4-cgnat.txt  traceroute -n -m 30 -w 1 8.8.8.8 from a home connection (private and CGNAT hops, three routers in one
                                 hop of 100.64.x.x, a silent hop, the destination)
  windows-ptbr-real-v4.txt       tracert -d -h 30 -w 1000 8.8.8.8, Windows in Portuguese (Brazil), CRLF, 13 hops, destination reached
  windows-ptbr-real-names.txt    tracert -h 12 -w 1000 8.8.8.8: a two-line header ("dns.google [8.8.8.8]", "com no maximo 12 saltos:"), the
                                 name of the first router in the form "router [192.168.0.1]", the destination not reached
  windows-ptbr-real-silent.txt   tracert -d -h 30 -w 1000 192.0.2.1: a hop with one lost probe among answers, then 26 hops with no
                                 answer ("Esgotado o tempo limite do pedido."), which the parser cuts at the end of the route
                                 (The three came from PowerShell as UTF-16 files and were converted to UTF-8; the text is the same.)
  linux-traceroute-v6-real.txt   traceroute -n -m 30 -w 1 -6 2001:4860:4860::8888 run on the server of a hosting provider, over IPv6
                                 (two and three routers in one hop, the destination at the end)

Written by hand from the documented format of each tool. They are NOT output of a real machine, and must be replaced by the real
ones when someone has them (the roadmap asks for tracert in pt-BR, en, es and sv; traceroute and traceroute6 of macOS):
  windows-ptbr-tracert.txt            tracert in Portuguese (CRLF), names in brackets, "Esgotado o tempo limite do pedido."
  windows-en-tracert-silent.txt       tracert in English, the destination answers, then two lines with no answer
  windows-es-tracert-unreachable.txt  tracert in Spanish, a router that reports "Host de destino inaccesible"
  macos-traceroute.txt                traceroute of macOS, with the routers of a hop in lines of their own
  macos-traceroute6.txt               traceroute6 of macOS (a link-local address with its zone)
  linux-traceroute-v6.txt             traceroute -6 -n of Linux
  linux-traceroute-30hops.txt         30 hops, three of them with no answer
  mtr-silent-alt.txt                  mtr with "???" (no "%" in that line, as mtr writes it) and a second router under a hop
  lsr1.txt                            the text of the app (LSR1)
The addresses are examples; the ones of internal networks are the usual ones (192.168.x.x, 10.x.x.x, 100.64.x.x).
Added after the review (hand-made too): linux-tracepath-reached.txt (the last line says "reached"), mtr-unreached.txt (the report ends in
"???"), macos-traceroute-multi.txt (a warning line with an address before the "traceroute to name (address)" line),
windows-ptbr-tracert-lines-only.txt (pasted with no header line). The mtr line of the second router of a hop is written as
"    |  `|-- address", which is how mtr writes it as far as the reviewer remembers: it is NOT confirmed by a run of mtr (the fixture
used to have a different form, which was only a guess).
